theory.intro:
An algebraic expression is built from **terms**, separated by addition. The number multiplying a pronumeral in a term is its **coefficient**, and a term that is just a number is the **constant term**.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},>=stealth]
\node (co) at (0,0) {$4$};
\node (pr) at (0.4,0) {$x$};
\node at (1.0,0) {$-$};
\node at (1.8,0) {$17$};
\draw (-0.2,0.3)--(-0.2,0.5)--(0.65,0.5)--(0.65,0.3);
\node at (0.225,0.85) {term};
\draw (0.75,0.3)--(0.75,0.55)--(2.15,0.55)--(2.15,0.3);
\draw (1.45,0.55)--(1.45,1.05);
\node at (1.45,1.35) {constant term};
\node[align=center] (cl) at (-0.8,-1.3) {coefficient\\of $x$};
\node (pl) at (1.7,-1.3) {pronumeral};
\draw[->] (cl.north)--(co.south);
\draw[->] (pl.north west)--(pr.south);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
